{
  "sample_id": "11968167_t1",
  "next_action_type": "CTNF",
  "claim_ground_evidence": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1,
        4,
        6,
        9
      ],
      "ground": "102",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "G5146XIGPPOPPY5"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The amended independent claims and the dependent claims 4 and 9 still recite a multilayer resistant film including an ITO conductive film, a printed silver conductor layer, a carbon layer, an insulation layer and an adhesive layer. The Office’s cited reference (Aufderheide, US 2005/0083307) discloses these layers and teaches printing carbon (conductive ink) over silver ink for protection. The amendments add a general recitation that the carbon layer “is arranged to overlay said printed silver conductor layer or said insulation layer,” but the prior art describes applying carbon over silver where protection is required (e.g., tail/trace ends). The amendment does not introduce a convincing structural or functional limitation that distinguishes over Aufderheide’s disclosure as a whole.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 4, 5, 6, 9, and 10 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by USPA 2005/0083307 to Aufderheide et al.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "The carbon conductive ink was printed - over the silver ink on the end of the tail to protect the traces from corrosion and abrasion\" in paragraph 0048.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        2,
        7
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "G5146XIGPPOPPY5"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Claims 2 and 7 further specify layer ordering (carbon overlaid on printed silver conductor layer; insulation overlaid the carbon layer, etc.). The Examiner's prior action explained that Aufderheide teaches the same materials and discloses printing carbon conductive ink over silver traces where protection is required. Given that the claimed materials and order are taught or would have been an obvious arrangement to a person of ordinary skill for corrosion protection, the 103 rejections are maintained.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claims 2, 3, 7, and 8 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over USPA 2005/0083307 to Aufderheide et al.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "carbon conductive ink may be screen printed over silver ink traces in areas where corrosion protection is required (paragraph [0048]).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        3,
        5,
        8,
        10
      ],
      "ground": "other",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "G5146XIGPPOPPY5"
      ],
      "status": "withdrawn",
      "rationale": "Applicant expressly canceled claims 3, 5, 8 and 10 in the amendment (document G5146XIGPPOPPY5). Canceled claims are not considered further.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "G5146XIGPPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claim 3 (Canceled).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "G5146XIGPPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claim 5 (Canceled)",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "G5146XIGPPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claim 8 (Canceled)",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "G5146XIGPPOPPY5",
          "support_span": "Claim 10 (Canceled)",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues the amended claims recite that the carbon layer is arranged to overlay the printed silver conductor layer or the insulation layer and that Aufderheide’s disclosure concerns transparent conductor patterns for operated zones while the claimed structure is for non-operated zones; applicant contends Aufderheide’s carbon-printing is limited to 'end of the tail' protection and thus does not teach the claimed arrangement.",
      "response": "The amendment and remarks were considered. Aufderheide’s disclosure, as relied upon in the earlier Office action, expressly teaches printing carbon conductive ink over silver ink traces to protect traces from corrosion and abrasion (paragraph [0048]). The claimed addition that the carbon layer “is arranged to overlay said printed silver conductor layer or said insulation layer” is a general formulation that encompasses the carbon-over-silver arrangement already taught by Aufderheide. Applicant’s argument that Aufderheide’s carbon is limited to tail ends does not persuasively distinguish the claimed subject matter because Aufderheide discloses the same materials and the practice of applying carbon over silver where protection is required; that teaching makes the carbon-over-silver arrangement available for use across traces/areas where corrosion protection is desirable. As submitted, the amendment and argument do not identify a structural or functional limitation that is absent from Aufderheide or that provides an unexpected result. Therefore, the anticipation and obviousness grounds remain.",
      "support_doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
      "support_span": "The carbon conductive ink was printed - over the silver ink on the end of the tail to protect the traces from corrosion and abrasion\" in paragraph 0048."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant states claims 4 and 9 are limitations of the independent claims reciting a specific layer order (printed silver on ITO, insulation overlaid printed silver, carbon overlaid insulation, adhesive overlaid carbon). Applicant contends Aufderheide does not teach the resistant film structure as recited.",
      "response": "Aufderheide describes ITO conductive films printed on substrates, silver conductor layers applied over the ITO, solvent-based epoxy insulator inks as insulator layers, and carbon conductive inks applied over silver ink traces for protection. The combination of these materials in the recited layer order would have been known to a person of ordinary skill and is either disclosed or rendered obvious by Aufderheide’s teachings about layering and protective carbon printing. Applicant’s contention that Aufderheide’s structure is limited to operated zones has not been supported with record evidence demonstrating a meaningful structural or functional difference for the claimed non-operated zones. Counsel and applicant may present evidence (e.g., a declaration, comparative data, or a persuasive legal argument about claim construction or technical differences) that demonstrates a non-obvious distinction or unexpected effect attributable to the specific ordering and arrangement recited; absent such evidence the 102/103 findings are maintained.",
      "support_doc_id": "G1DP23M1PPOPPY5",
      "support_span": "A silver conductor layer was overlaid on the surface of the ITO layer (paragraph [0045]). An insulator (solvent-based epoxy insulator ink) was overlaid on the printed silver conductor layer (paragraph [0046]). An carbon conductive ink may be screen printed over silver ink traces (paragraph [0048])."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "SUMMARY The Office has considered the applicant’s January 25, 2010 amendment and remarks (documents G5146XBDPPOPPY5, G5146XGHPPOPPY5, G5146XGWPPOPPY5, G5146XHSPPOPPY5, G5146XIGPPOPPY5 and G5146XITPPOPPY5). The amendment cancels claims 3, 5, 8 and 10 and presents amended independent claims 1 and 6 and dependent claims 2, 4, 7 and 9. The prior Non-Final Office action of October 29, 2009 (document G1DP23M1PPOPPY5) relied upon US 2005/0083307 (Aufderheide) for rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102 and 103. After review, the Examiner finds the amendment and applicant’s remarks do not overcome the cited rejections for the reasons below. Rejections maintained (summary): - Claims 1, 4, 6 and 9 — Rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as anticipated by Aufderheide (US 2005/0083307). - Claims 2 and 7 — Rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as unpatentable over Aufderheide. Canceled claims: 3, 5, 8 and 10 have been canceled by applicant and are not considered further. ANALYSIS OF THE AMENDMENT AND REMARKS 1) Anticipation (35 U.S.C. 102) — claims 1, 4, 6, 9 Applicant amended the independent claims to add that the carbon layer “is arranged to overlay said printed silver conductor layer or said insulation layer for avoiding dissociation of silver ions from said printed silver layer.” The Office previously explained and the record supports that Aufderheide discloses multilayer resistive touch constructions with ITO conductive films, printed silver conductors and carbon conductive inks, as well as insulating and adhesive layers. The prior action notes and applicant’s own remarks draw attention to Aufderheide’s teaching that carbon conductive ink can be printed over silver ink “on the end of the tail to protect the traces from corrosion and abrasion” (see the record citation). That teaching shows application of carbon over silver for protection — the same arrangement recited in the amended independent claims. The newly added language is a general functional statement (preventing silver dissociation) and does not identify a structural limitation or a specific arrangement that is absent from Aufderheide. Consequently, the elements as amended remain disclosed or enabled by Aufderheide and the anticipation rejection is maintained for the recited claims. 2) Obviousness (35 U.S.C. 103) — claims 2 and 7 Claims 2 and 7 further limit the layer order (printed silver on ITO; carbon overlaid on printed silver; insulation overlaid the carbon layer; adhesive overlaid the insulation layer, etc.). Aufderheide describes ITO conductive films, application of silver conductors thereon, use of solvent-based epoxy insulating inks, and screen-printing carbon conductive ink over silver traces to protect them. Given these teachings, arranging the listed materials in the claimed order for corrosion protection and device fabrication would have been within the routine skill of one of ordinary skill in the art. Applicant’s remarks point to distinctions based on “operated zones” vs. “non-operated zones” and the alleged limitation that Aufderheide’s carbon printing is limited to trace tail ends. Those arguments are noted but are not supported by record evidence showing a technical distinction or unexpected result that would overcome obviousness. Therefore, the 103 rejections for claims 2 and 7 are maintained. 3) Applicant’s arguments about distinctions and amendments Applicant argues that the claimed carbon arrangement is for non-operated zones and that Aufderheide’s carbon teaching pertains only to tail ends for protection. The record shows Aufderheide expressly contemplates printing carbon conductive ink over silver ink traces where corrosion protection is required (paragraph [0048]), and teaches suitable substrates (plastic or glass) and insulating/adhesive materials. Applicant has not submitted factual evidence (e.g., comparative data, expert declaration, or persuasive claim construction showing the claimed “non-operated zones” limitation excludes Aufderheide’s disclosure) to overcome the rejections. 4) Drawings/Abstract/Specification corrections Applicant submitted corrected text for paragraph [0020], an amended abstract, and replacement drawing sheets (remarks indicate Figures 9-11 bracket removal and correction of electrode numbering in the specification). The corrections to the specification and abstract respond to the earlier objections; the drawing objection under 37 C.F.R. 1.84(p)(5) and 1.84(p)(1) is considered in light of the submitted replacement drawing sheets. If the drawing replacement sheets fully correct the earlier identified deficiencies, the drawing objections will be withdrawn. If the Examiner identifies discrepancies upon formal review of the replacement sheets, a subsequent Office action will notify applicant. (Note: review of the replacement drawings on file is in progress; any remaining formal drawing defects will be raised in a subsequent communication.) 5) Priority documents Applicant previously asserted foreign priority (Taiwanese application filed January 17, 2007). The record contains a note that a certified copy of the foreign priority application has not been filed as required by 35 U.S.C. 119(b). If priority is to be relied upon, applicant should submit the certified copy as required. OPTIONS TO ADVANCE PROSECUTION To overcome the maintained rejections applicant may consider one or more of the following: - Amend the claims to recite specific, limiting structural or process features that are not taught or suggested by Aufderheide (for example, limited geometry, placement, manufacturing steps, compositions, thicknesses, or a functional limitation tied to a structural feature that yields an unexpected technical advantage). Broad functional language that reads on the same protection use taught in Aufderheide will not distinguish the claims. - Provide persuasive evidence and explanation (e.g., declaration under 37 C.F.R. 1.132, comparative data) that the claimed arrangement achieves an unexpected result or a technical advantage not taught or suggested by Aufderheide. Evidence should establish facts not apparent from the prior art itself. - Argue claim construction and point to explicit, supported distinctions in the specification and drawings that tie the claimed subject matter to features absent from Aufderheide. Present clear record citations if relying on the specification to interpret claim terms. - If applicant contends Aufderheide’s disclosure is nonanalogous or does not teach the claimed elements, point to the specific passages in Aufderheide (with copies or highlighted citations) and provide a legal and factual explanation why those teachings do not anticipate or render obvious the claimed subject matter. NEXT ACTION Applicant is given ONE MONTH from the mailing date of this communication to reply with amendments and/or arguments and/or evidence. Absent a persuasive reply that overcomes the cited rejections, the Examiner will issue a further Office action (which may be final) addressing any remaining claims and issues. The applicant should also ensure submission of the certified foreign priority document if priority is to be maintained. Limitations: this communication relies on the record documents available through the A1 date. The Examiner’s citations to Aufderheide are based on the Office action in the record (document G1DP23M1PPOPPY5). If applicant submits new evidence or claim changes not yet in the record, the Examiner will consider them upon timely reply.",
  "limitations": [
    "The Examiner’s characterization of Aufderheide is taken from the Office action already in the record (G1DP23M1PPOPPY5). The full text of Aufderheide is not reproduced here; the Examiner relied on the prior action’s quotations when applying 102/103.",
    "Acceptance or formal sufficiency of the replacement drawing sheets is subject to formal review of those sheets on file; this communication summarizes the correction asserted by applicant but does not represent a final acceptance of the replacement drawings until formal review is complete.",
    "The predicted A1_state's indication that amended claims were 'currently_amended' is treated as applicant-submitted amendments (document G5146XIGPPOPPY5). If the actual file-wrapper status differs, applicant should notify the Office and provide the appropriate file document."
  ]
}

