  "office_action": "Response to Applicant's After-Final/Amendment Filing (A1) — Non-Final Examiner Action\nSummary of status\n- Applicant's A1 filing (dated 2011-11-29, doc GVNXDYBTPXXIFW1) cancels claims 1–13 and submits an amendment to claim 14. The cancellations are entered and those cancelled claims are no longer before the Office. (GVNXDYBTPXXIFW1: \"1 to 13 (Cancelled).\")\n- Corrected formal drawings were submitted on 2011-12-13 (GW5F0MGKPXXIFW3). The drawing objections in the August 1, 2011 Office action are considered addressed by the corrected replacement sheet and the drawing objections are therefore withdrawn.\n- The Examiner has reviewed the amended claim 14 and the arguments presented in the Applicant's Remarks. The Examiner maintains the prior rejection of claim 14 under 35 U.S.C. 103 for the reasons set forth below, but notes a formal objection that the amended claim text contains a typographical/formatting error that must be corrected.\nClaim-level disposition and reasoning\n1) Claims 1–13\nStatus: Withdrawn from prosecution (cancelled by applicant).\nEvidence: Applicant's A1 (GVNXDYBTPXXIFW1) expressly states \"1 to 13 (Cancelled).\" As cancelled claims, any rejections previously asserted against those claims are terminated as to them.\n2) Claim 14 (presently amended)\nStatus: Rejection under 35 U.S.C. 103 maintained (non-final).\nClaim text on record (A1, GVNXDYBTPXXIFW1):\n\"A personal reminder digital voice recorder comprising: a) a battery operated audio microchip system for recording, storing and reproducing a single personal audio recording on each of a plurality of channels; b) a single playback button which can operate each of the plurality of channels; c) an a sing le LED corresponding to each of the channels, each LED having a means to turn the LED on when the channel has an active message recorded; and d) a means to turn each LED off when a corresponding channel has a message that is inactive.\" (entered as the currently amended claim)\nExaminer's reasoning: The prior Office action (GQO3GTZ0PPOPPY5, dated 2011-08-01) relied on the Multi Message Recorder (MMR) product literature (as cited) to show a personal multi-channel voice recorder with per-channel stored messages and per-channel LED indication, and relied on Pawlowski (US 6,038,199) to show user interfaces that select among multiple stored voice files and play selected files using common playback controls. The combination of these teachings provides the elements recited in amended claim 14: the MMR teaching supplies the multiple channels, per-channel LEDs and LED behavior, while Pawlowski supplies the teaching of selecting stored messages for playback using a single playback control, thereby rendering a device with \"a single playback button which can operate each of the plurality of channels\" obvious for the stated purpose of reducing interface complexity while maintaining channel indication and playback capability. The earlier Office action specifically stated the ground for combining these teachings (GQO3GTZ0PPOPPY5: \"Claim 14 is rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over 'MMR' ... in view of Pawlowski et al. ('199).\").\nApplicant's argument and Examiner response: Applicant argues that the references do not teach or suggest \"a single playback button with multiple LEDs\" and that if such a combination were obvious it would have been either stated or manufactured. The Examiner has considered the argument but finds it unpersuasive. The combination is reasoned: MMR teaches per-channel message storage and channel-specific LED indicators; Pawlowski teaches selection among stored voice files and common playback controls. It would have been within the routine skill of a person having ordinary skill in the field to combine these features to produce a compact personal recorder that uses a single playback control to play any selected channel while retaining per-channel LEDs to indicate message status. Applicant has not submitted documentary evidence that the combination is technically infeasible, nor evidence that either reference teaches away from combining the single-playback-control selection feature with per-channel visual indicators. Therefore the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection of claim 14 is maintained.\nFormal objection to claim form\nThe amendment filed with the A1 (GVNXDYBTPXXIFW1) contains a typographical/formatting error in the claim text: the phrase \"an a sing le LED\" appears where the intended phrase appears to be \"an LED\" or \"a single LED\"/\"an LED corresponding to each of the channels\". This typographical defect renders the claim text unclear and must be corrected. The applicant is required to submit a clean corrected claim in which the typographical error is fixed (e.g., \"an LED corresponding to each of the channels\"), under 37 C.F.R. 1.121(b) (amendments to the specification and claims after filing) or by an appropriate cancellation/replacement of the claim language. If the applicant chooses to submit a corrected claim, please also ensure that the corrected claim text is consistent with the intended scope as argued in the Remarks. The correction is a formality — once corrected, prosecution on the merits will continue with the maintained 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection unless applicant provides persuasive substantive rebuttal or submits further claim amendments to distinguish over the applied prior art.\nDrawings\nCorrected replacement drawings were submitted on 2011-12-13 (GW5F0MGKPXXIFW3). The drawing objections identified in the August 1, 2011 action are considered addressed by the replacement sheet and are withdrawn.\nWhat the applicant can do next\n- To overcome the maintained 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection of claim 14, applicant may: (1) submit an amendment to claim 14 that adds limitations not suggested by the applied references and which distinguish over the combination of MMR and Pawlowski (with explanation), or (2) submit persuasive arguments explaining why the combination of MMR and Pawlowski would not render the claimed subject matter obvious — for example, by pointing to specific passages in the cited references that teach away from the combination or by presenting evidence of unexpected results, teaching away, or other objective indicia of nonobviousness.\n- Applicant must also correct the typographical/formatting error in the claim text (e.g., amend the phrase \"an a sing le LED\" to the intended phrase) so that the claim is clear and in proper form; submit the corrected claim language as an amendment under 37 C.F.R. 1.121(b). If applicant files such a correction, include an explanation if the correction changes claim scope in any way — otherwise indicate that the correction is a nonsubstantive typographical correction.\n- If applicant chooses to further amend claim 14, please explain how the amendment changes patentability with respect to the cited references (MMR and Pawlowski).\nDecision\nThe pending rejection of claim 14 under 35 U.S.C. 103 is maintained. Claims 1–13 are cancelled by applicant and are no longer before the Office. The drawing objections from the August 1, 2011 Office action are withdrawn based on the replacement drawings filed December 13, 2011 (GW5F0MGKPXXIFW3). Applicant must correct the typographical defect in the amended claim text and may then either respond with substantive arguments/evidence or propose further claim amendments to address the maintained rejection.\nSincerely,\nExamining Corps (Art Unit 2614)\n(Technical analysis and citations are based on the record in the application file, including GQO3GTZ0PPOPPY5 and the applicant submissions.)",
