  "office_action": "Examiner Remarks and Action - Non-Final Office Action\nSummary\nThe applicant filed an amendment and remarks by facsimile on June 16, 2008 (documents FHKTFGTIPPOPPY5, FHKTFGUFPPOPPY5, FHKTFGYPPPOPPY5). The previously-mailed Examiner's Office action dated June 9, 2008 (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5) is the controlling action for the current status of examination because the submitted amended claims have not yet been entered into the official application file. Accordingly, the rejections and objections in the June 9, 2008 Office action are continued as set forth below.\nProcedural note regarding the submitted amendment\nThe facsimile submission (FHKTFGUFPPOPPY5) and remarks (FHKTFGYPPPOPPY5) are of record as received on June 16, 2008. The submission indicates that claims 1-20 are amended and that the applicant adopted the examiner's suggested claim language changes. However, the file currently contains only the claims as examined in the June 9, 2008 Office action. The examiner cannot treat the amendments as entered unless and until the applicant provides a formal amendment in proper format (37 C.F.R. § 1.121) or otherwise requests entry and the file is updated accordingly. To place the amended claims of FHKTFGUFPPOPPY5 into the record as the operative claims, please provide one of the following within two months from the mailing date of this communication: (A) a clean, full claim set in which each claim is presented in full and is expressly identified as replacing the prior claims, accompanied by a statement requesting entry under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121; or (B) a confirmation that the facsimile-submitted claim text is to be entered, along with any necessary corrected pages if legibility issues exist. Absent such entry, the examiner will continue prosecution against the claims as they were examined on June 9, 2008.\nClaims and Grounds Remaining in Effect (for claims as examined on 06/09/2008)\n1) 35 U.S.C. 112, second paragraph: Claims 19 and 20\n- Status: maintained for the claims as previously examined. The June 9, 2008 Office action states: \"Claims 19 and 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112, second paragraph, as being indefinite\" (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5). Applicant's remarks state claim 19 was amended to depend from claim 14; that amendment, if formally entered, would address the antecedent-basis problem for \"said spring.\" Until the amendment is entered, the 112 rejection remains for the previously-examined claims.\nAction requested: Submit the formal replacement claim set or confirm entry so that the examiner may enter the amendment and withdraw the 112 rejection if appropriate.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 102(b) and 35 U.S.C. 103(a): Claims 1, 2, 5, 6, 14, 15, 18, 19 (102/103) and Claims 3, 4, 7-13, 16, 17, 20 (103)\n- Status: maintained for the claims as previously examined. The June 9, 2008 Office action sets forth rejections based on Aihara et al. (102/103) and Aihara in view of Ogishi (103) for the dependent claims (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5). The examiner had also provided claim wording suggestions which, if adopted and entered, were indicated as likely to render claims allowable. Applicant asserts the suggested changes have been made; again, entry is required to enable the examiner to determine whether the prior art rejections are overcome by the exact amended claim language.\nAction requested: If the applicant believes the June 9, 2008 rejections are overcome by the submitted claims, ensure the proposed claims are properly entered as the official claim set. If the applicant prefers that the examiner reconsider the rejections without entry, provide explicit, point-by-point arguments explaining why Aihara (and Ogishi where applied) do not teach or render obvious the claimed subject matter, referencing specific passages of the cited references. Simply stating that the claims were amended per the \"Allowable Subject Matter\" paragraph is insufficient to have the examiner withdraw the cited rejections until the amended claims are of record and examined.\nExemplar evidence from the file\n- \"Claims 19 and 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112, second paragraph, as being indefinite\" (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5).\n- \"Claims 1, 2, 5, 6, 14, 15, 18, and 19 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as anticipated by ... Aihara et al.\" (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5).\n- \"Claims 3, 4, 7-13, 16, 17, and 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Aihara et al in view of Ogishi.\" (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5).\nExaminer's response to applicant remarks\nThe examiner appreciates the applicant's efforts and the prompt faxed response. If the applicant intended the faxed amended claims to be entered, the examiner cannot locate a clear replacement claim set that expressly replaces the prior claims in the file wrapper. The examiner therefore requests formal entry as described above. Once the amendment is entered, the examiner will promptly re-evaluate the 112 and the 102/103 rejections in light of the entered claim language and the remarks provided. If entry is confirmed and the examiner determines that certain rejections are overcome, the examiner will cancel the appropriate rejections and move toward allowance or, if appropriate, issue a subsequent Office action (possible Notice of Allowability or other action) explaining any remaining objections or rejections.\nSummary of required applicant action (to avoid continued rejections based on previously-examined claims)\n1) Submit a clear, full claim set in which each claim is presented in full and is expressly identified as replacing the prior claims, with a request for entry under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121; or confirm and correct the facsimile-submitted claims so they may be entered as the official claims. 2) If the applicant prefers to keep the existing claim text (as examined 06/09/2008), file substantive arguments directed to the specific limitations that the examiner relied upon in applying Aihara and Ogishi, referencing specific passages of the prior art, so the examiner can respond on the merits.\nTime period for reply\nA response that provides the requested formal replacement claim set or the requested arguments should be filed within two months from the mailing date of this communication. Failure to respond within the time period will result in continuation of the outstanding rejections and may result in further action up to and including a final rejection.\nLimitations and procedural notes\n- The examiner's statements about what Aihara et al. and Ogishi disclose are drawn from the June 9, 2008 Office action (FH9QDKKQPPOPPY5). Applicant's amendments, while filed by fax (FHKTFGUFPPOPPY5), are not yet confirmed entered; thus the examiner cannot yet treat those claims as the operative claims for determining patentability. - If there are any legibility or transmission issues with the facsimile submission, please re-submit by mail or through EFS-Web (as applicable) a clear replacement claim set and identify that submission as correcting the prior facsimile.\nConclusion\nThe Office action mailed June 9, 2008 is continued-in-effect for the claim text as examined on that date. The examiner recognizes applicant's intent to adopt the examiner's suggested claim wording and to correct the 112 defect for claim 19. To obtain reconsideration on the basis of the amended claims, the applicant must have those claims entered into the record. Upon entry, the examiner will promptly reconsider all outstanding rejections and objections in view of the entered claims and applicant remarks.",
